1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,046
The Bank of England has just put interest rates

2
00:00:03,046 --> 00:00:04,798
up by a quarter of a per cent.

3
00:00:04,798 --> 00:00:08,011
On a two-hundred-thousand-pound mortgage, that can mean

4
00:00:08,011 --> 00:00:10,523
about twenty-nine pounds more, every month.

5
00:00:10,523 --> 00:00:12,859
Here's where that number comes from, why

6
00:00:12,859 --> 00:00:15,138
it happened, and who actually feels it.

7
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:21,680
Last time, we saw how a mortgage is built.

8
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:25,399
Today, why rates change, and what a change actually does.

9
00:00:25,399 --> 00:00:28,595
We'll follow one rate change all the way through.

10
00:00:28,595 --> 00:00:31,139
Why the Bank of England made it, how it

11
00:00:31,139 --> 00:00:33,945
travels to your lender, and what it does to

12
00:00:33,945 --> 00:00:37,141
your mortgage, your savings and your investments.

13
00:00:37,141 --> 00:00:40,729
By the end, you'll know what changes for you, and when.

14
00:00:40,729 --> 00:00:43,861
As always, this is a simple example, not advice.

15
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:47,997
So first, why did the Bank raise rates at all?

16
00:00:47,997 --> 00:00:51,062
Its job is to keep inflation, the speed at which

17
00:00:51,062 --> 00:00:53,744
prices rise, close to two per cent a year.

18
00:00:53,744 --> 00:00:56,809
Inflation is measured by comparing the cost of a

19
00:00:56,809 --> 00:00:59,937
typical basket of things people buy, from food to

20
00:00:59,937 --> 00:01:02,108
fuel to rent, with a year earlier.

21
00:01:02,108 --> 00:01:06,067
In September, that basket cost three and a half per cent more.

22
00:01:06,067 --> 00:01:08,365
Picture a hundred-pound weekly shop.

23
00:01:08,365 --> 00:01:10,664
A year later, the same trolley costs

24
00:01:10,664 --> 00:01:12,771
a hundred and three pounds fifty.

25
00:01:12,771 --> 00:01:14,623
A big part of that is energy.

26
00:01:14,623 --> 00:01:17,368
Oil and gas have climbed sharply this year,

27
00:01:17,368 --> 00:01:20,306
and that feeds into fuel, household bills, and

28
00:01:20,306 --> 00:01:22,668
the cost of making almost everything.

29
00:01:22,668 --> 00:01:25,988
The Bank expects inflation to keep rising, to around

30
00:01:25,988 --> 00:01:28,606
five per cent by the middle of next year.

31
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:33,680
Raising interest rates is its main tool for slowing that down.

32
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:37,060
When borrowing costs more and saving pays more, people

33
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:39,751
and businesses tend to spend a little less.

34
00:01:39,751 --> 00:01:42,504
Less spending means less pressure on prices.

35
00:01:42,504 --> 00:01:45,133
Think of it as easing off the accelerator.

36
00:01:45,133 --> 00:01:48,825
The car doesn't stop straight away, but it slows over time.

37
00:01:48,825 --> 00:01:51,015
That's why a rate change can take a

38
00:01:51,015 --> 00:01:53,206
year or more to work through fully.

39
00:01:54,410 --> 00:01:57,486
The decision is made by the Bank's Monetary Policy

40
00:01:57,486 --> 00:02:00,623
Committee, nine people who meet eight times a year.

41
00:02:00,623 --> 00:02:03,576
On the fifth of November, eight of them voted to

42
00:02:03,576 --> 00:02:06,221
raise Bank Rate by a quarter of a per cent,

43
00:02:06,221 --> 00:02:08,989
from three point seven five to four per cent.

44
00:02:08,989 --> 00:02:12,311
The ninth wanted to go further, to four and a quarter.

45
00:02:12,311 --> 00:02:15,264
It was the first rise since twenty twenty-three.

46
00:02:15,264 --> 00:02:17,540
Bank Rate is what the Bank pays banks

47
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:19,447
on the money they keep with it.

48
00:02:19,447 --> 00:02:23,876
So when it rises, borrowing gets more expensive right across the system.

49
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:28,573
For context, five years ago Bank Rate was close to zero.

50
00:02:28,573 --> 00:02:31,317
It climbed to five and a quarter per cent in

51
00:02:31,317 --> 00:02:35,246
twenty twenty-three, the highest in fifteen years, and had been

52
00:02:35,246 --> 00:02:38,489
cut back to three point seven five before this rise.

53
00:02:38,489 --> 00:02:41,669
So four per cent isn't high by history's standards.

54
00:02:41,669 --> 00:02:44,414
What matters is the change of direction, and

55
00:02:44,414 --> 00:02:46,971
that's what lenders and markets react to.

56
00:02:48,170 --> 00:02:50,782
Think of it as a tap at the top of a pipe.

57
00:02:50,782 --> 00:02:52,772
Turn it up, and the change flows

58
00:02:52,772 --> 00:02:54,824
down to lenders, and then to you.

59
00:02:54,824 --> 00:02:57,809
But it doesn't reach everyone at the same speed.

60
00:02:59,010 --> 00:03:01,500
If your mortgage is a tracker, it follows

61
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:04,597
Bank Rate directly, usually from your next payment.

62
00:03:04,597 --> 00:03:07,087
Trackers are Bank Rate plus a set margin,

63
00:03:07,087 --> 00:03:08,969
say one and a quarter per cent.

64
00:03:08,969 --> 00:03:11,945
So three point seven five gave you five per cent,

65
00:03:11,945 --> 00:03:14,495
and four now gives you five and a quarter.

66
00:03:14,495 --> 00:03:17,349
A standard variable rate is set by your lender.

67
00:03:17,349 --> 00:03:19,960
It usually follows, but it doesn't have to,

68
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:22,025
and not always by the same amount.

69
00:03:22,025 --> 00:03:26,458
Take two hundred thousand pounds over twenty-five years at five per cent.

70
00:03:26,458 --> 00:03:31,255
That's one thousand, one hundred and sixty-nine pounds, eighteen pence a month.

71
00:03:31,255 --> 00:03:33,442
At five and a quarter per cent, it's

72
00:03:33,442 --> 00:03:36,903
one thousand, one hundred and ninety-eight pounds, fifty.

73
00:03:36,903 --> 00:03:39,818
Twenty-nine pounds, thirty-two more every month.

74
00:03:39,818 --> 00:03:43,158
That's roughly two takeaway coffees a week, every week,

75
00:03:43,158 --> 00:03:45,344
for as long as the rate stays there.

76
00:03:45,344 --> 00:03:48,016
About three hundred and fifty pounds a year.

77
00:03:49,210 --> 00:03:50,652
And over the whole loan?

78
00:03:50,652 --> 00:03:53,115
Remember from last time, at five per cent

79
00:03:53,115 --> 00:03:55,938
this mortgage costs about one hundred and fifty

80
00:03:55,938 --> 00:03:58,522
thousand, eight hundred pounds in interest.

81
00:03:58,522 --> 00:04:00,444
If the rate stayed at five and a

82
00:04:00,444 --> 00:04:03,328
quarter for all twenty-five years, that would be

83
00:04:03,328 --> 00:04:06,692
about one hundred and fifty-nine thousand, five hundred.

84
00:04:06,692 --> 00:04:09,636
Nearly eight thousand, eight hundred pounds more.

85
00:04:09,636 --> 00:04:11,678
That's about the price of a decent

86
00:04:11,678 --> 00:04:13,661
used car, from one quarter point.

87
00:04:14,860 --> 00:04:16,635
Here's a handy rule of thumb.

88
00:04:16,635 --> 00:04:21,409
On a twenty-five-year repayment mortgage, each quarter-point rise adds roughly

89
00:04:21,409 --> 00:04:24,960
fifteen pounds a month for every hundred thousand you owe.

90
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:27,959
So about twenty-two pounds on a hundred and fifty

91
00:04:27,959 --> 00:04:31,448
thousand, and about forty-four on three hundred thousand.

92
00:04:32,650 --> 00:04:35,515
If you're on a fixed deal, nothing changes today.

93
00:04:35,515 --> 00:04:38,029
Your payment is locked until the deal ends.

94
00:04:38,029 --> 00:04:40,894
But lenders price new fixed deals partly on where

95
00:04:40,894 --> 00:04:43,408
they expect rates to go, so new fixes often

96
00:04:43,408 --> 00:04:45,630
get dearer before the Bank even moves.

97
00:04:45,630 --> 00:04:48,203
Picture someone who fixed at four and a half

98
00:04:48,203 --> 00:04:50,541
per cent two years ago on the same loan.

99
00:04:50,541 --> 00:04:53,406
They've been paying one thousand, one hundred and

100
00:04:53,406 --> 00:04:56,037
eleven pounds, sixty-six a month, and now owe

101
00:04:56,037 --> 00:04:58,493
about one hundred and ninety-one thousand.

102
00:04:58,493 --> 00:05:00,481
If the best rate on offer today is

103
00:05:00,481 --> 00:05:02,820
five and a quarter, their new payment is

104
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:06,094
about one thousand, one hundred and ninety-three pounds.

105
00:05:06,094 --> 00:05:08,608
That's eighty-one pounds a month more, from

106
00:05:08,608 --> 00:05:11,181
changes that happened while they were fixed.

107
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:14,301
So here's a question for you.

108
00:05:14,301 --> 00:05:18,540
Is your mortgage fixed or variable, and when does your deal end?

109
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:20,129
Tell me in the comments.

110
00:05:20,129 --> 00:05:22,646
It decides when this rise reaches you.

111
00:05:23,850 --> 00:05:25,473
And if rates keep climbing?

112
00:05:25,473 --> 00:05:27,756
The Bank has said more rises may come.

113
00:05:27,756 --> 00:05:30,341
Each extra quarter point adds about another

114
00:05:30,341 --> 00:05:32,564
thirty pounds a month on our example.

115
00:05:32,564 --> 00:05:34,968
At five and a half per cent, the payment

116
00:05:34,968 --> 00:05:38,814
is one thousand, two hundred and twenty-eight pounds, seventeen.

117
00:05:38,814 --> 00:05:40,918
At six per cent, it's one thousand,

118
00:05:40,918 --> 00:05:43,502
two hundred and eighty-eight pounds, sixty.

119
00:05:43,502 --> 00:05:46,207
That's nearly one hundred and twenty pounds a

120
00:05:46,207 --> 00:05:48,190
month more than at five per cent.

121
00:05:48,190 --> 00:05:52,036
Over a year, about one thousand, four hundred and thirty pounds.

122
00:05:53,230 --> 00:05:54,528
So what happens next?

123
00:05:54,528 --> 00:05:57,062
The October inflation figure lands on the

124
00:05:57,062 --> 00:05:59,720
eighteenth of November, and the Bank's next

125
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:02,378
decision is on the seventeenth of December.

126
00:06:02,378 --> 00:06:06,458
If prices are still speeding up, another rise becomes more likely.

127
00:06:06,458 --> 00:06:08,435
If they cool, the Bank may hold.

128
00:06:08,435 --> 00:06:10,784
Either way, we'll cover it on the day.

129
00:06:11,990 --> 00:06:14,571
Savers sit at the other end of the pipe.

130
00:06:14,571 --> 00:06:17,539
Ten thousand pounds earning a quarter of a per

131
00:06:17,539 --> 00:06:20,958
cent more makes about twenty-five pounds more a year.

132
00:06:20,958 --> 00:06:23,023
But this tap often drips slowly.

133
00:06:23,023 --> 00:06:25,023
Banks don't have to pass a rise

134
00:06:25,023 --> 00:06:26,894
on, and many take their time.

135
00:06:26,894 --> 00:06:30,443
Easy-access accounts can change their rate at any time.

136
00:06:30,443 --> 00:06:32,959
Fixed-term savings lock a rate in, so a

137
00:06:32,959 --> 00:06:35,475
fix taken before the rise won't get it.

138
00:06:35,475 --> 00:06:38,121
New fixed-term deals, though, tend to pay

139
00:06:38,121 --> 00:06:39,863
a little more after a rise.

140
00:06:41,060 --> 00:06:42,310
There's a catch, too.

141
00:06:42,310 --> 00:06:44,571
With prices rising at three and a half

142
00:06:44,571 --> 00:06:46,951
per cent, savings need to earn more than

143
00:06:46,951 --> 00:06:48,736
that just to hold their value.

144
00:06:48,736 --> 00:06:51,176
Ten thousand pounds earning four per cent

145
00:06:51,176 --> 00:06:53,318
makes four hundred pounds in a year.

146
00:06:53,318 --> 00:06:56,294
Take away three and a half per cent inflation, and

147
00:06:56,294 --> 00:06:59,626
in real terms you're only about fifty pounds better off.

148
00:06:59,626 --> 00:07:01,888
It's like walking up a down escalator.

149
00:07:01,888 --> 00:07:05,874
You're moving, but the stairs are carrying you back almost as fast.

150
00:07:05,874 --> 00:07:09,742
Earn less than inflation, and your savings are quietly shrinking.

151
00:07:10,940 --> 00:07:12,623
What about other borrowing?

152
00:07:12,623 --> 00:07:15,740
Most personal loans and car finance are fixed when

153
00:07:15,740 --> 00:07:18,857
you take them out, so existing deals don't change.

154
00:07:18,857 --> 00:07:21,600
Credit card rates already sit far above Bank

155
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:23,969
Rate, so a quarter point barely shows.

156
00:07:23,969 --> 00:07:26,836
New borrowing can get a little dearer, though.

157
00:07:26,836 --> 00:07:29,143
And if you rent, you won't see a rate

158
00:07:29,143 --> 00:07:32,322
on your bill, but landlords with mortgages feel it,

159
00:07:32,322 --> 00:07:34,816
and over time that can show up in rents.

160
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:38,767
Investments feel it too, in a different way.

161
00:07:38,767 --> 00:07:41,077
Say you own a ten-year bond that pays

162
00:07:41,077 --> 00:07:43,824
four pounds a year for every hundred pounds.

163
00:07:43,824 --> 00:07:46,945
If new bonds start paying four pounds twenty-five,

164
00:07:46,945 --> 00:07:49,629
nobody will pay the full hundred for yours.

165
00:07:49,629 --> 00:07:52,439
Its price drops to about ninety-eight pounds,

166
00:07:52,439 --> 00:07:54,624
so its return matches the new ones.

167
00:07:54,624 --> 00:07:57,683
Another quarter point, and it's about ninety-six.

168
00:07:57,683 --> 00:07:59,618
The flip side is that new money

169
00:07:59,618 --> 00:08:01,616
going into bonds now earns more.

170
00:08:01,616 --> 00:08:05,486
Shares can wobble too, as borrowing gets dearer for companies.

171
00:08:05,486 --> 00:08:09,169
That's part of why markets can jump around on decision day.

172
00:08:10,370 --> 00:08:11,960
And it isn't just the UK.

173
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:14,694
In late October, the US Federal Reserve and

174
00:08:14,694 --> 00:08:17,745
the European Central Bank both held their rates,

175
00:08:17,745 --> 00:08:20,861
and markets are watching their December meetings.

176
00:08:20,861 --> 00:08:23,849
Wherever you live, the pipe works the same way.

177
00:08:25,050 --> 00:08:28,183
So what's worth knowing about your own position?

178
00:08:28,183 --> 00:08:29,032
Three things.

179
00:08:29,032 --> 00:08:31,512
First, what kind of mortgage you have,

180
00:08:31,512 --> 00:08:33,340
and the date your deal ends.

181
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:35,755
Second, the rate on your savings, and

182
00:08:35,755 --> 00:08:37,843
whether it moved after the rise.

183
00:08:37,843 --> 00:08:41,172
Third, if you invest, that bond prices dipping when

184
00:08:41,172 --> 00:08:44,109
rates rise is normal, and how much depends on

185
00:08:44,109 --> 00:08:46,329
how long the bond has left to run.

186
00:08:46,329 --> 00:08:47,830
None of that is advice.

187
00:08:47,830 --> 00:08:50,049
It's just knowing where you stand.

188
00:08:51,250 --> 00:08:54,296
So, a quarter-point rise looks small on paper.

189
00:08:54,296 --> 00:08:59,063
On a variable mortgage, it's about twenty-nine pounds a month, and soon.

190
00:08:59,063 --> 00:09:02,109
On a fixed deal, nothing, until the deal ends.

191
00:09:02,109 --> 00:09:04,757
For savers, a little more, often slowly,

192
00:09:04,757 --> 00:09:06,810
and it needs to beat inflation.

193
00:09:06,810 --> 00:09:09,326
Next week: what happens when you pay a

194
00:09:09,326 --> 00:09:12,107
little extra off your mortgage each month.

195
00:09:12,107 --> 00:09:14,159
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
